BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iwmf-kari-howard-fund-narrative-journalism-2026@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T204152Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: IWMF: The Kari Howard Fund for Narrative Journalism 2026
DESCRIPTION:Established in memory of editor Kari Howard\, who championed na
 rrative journalism that wove the music of everyday life into stories illum
 inating the most important issues of the day. Offered by Kari's family and
  friends together with the International Women's Media Foundation\, the fu
 nd supports narrative journalism projects. Grants average around USD 5\,00
 0 (based on previous years). ELIGIBILITY: open to women and nonbinary jour
 nalists. Applicants may be a print journalist or a print journalist leadin
 g a multi-media team\, and may be freelance or staff\; they may apply indi
 vidually or as part of a multi-format team. Professional journalism must b
 e the applicant's primary profession\, with three or more years of profess
 ional experience (internships do not count). Applicants must show proof of
  interest from an editor or a proven track record of publication in promin
 ent media outlets. Applicants must apply and publish in English\; projects
  published anywhere in the world are eligible\, and stories may be publish
 ed digitally (not necessarily in a newspaper or magazine). Any multi-forma
 t reporting must supplement a printed project. All reporting and publishin
 g must be completed within six months of the award. Applicants receive a d
 ecision by early September. DEADLINE: 12 July 2026\, 11:59pm EST.\n\nOrgan
 isation: International Women's Media Foundation (IWMF)\nLocation: Worldwid
 e\; projects reported and published in English anywhere in the world. Open
  to women and nonbinary journalists.\nAward: Grants average around USD 5\,
 000 (based on previous years). No application fee.\nDuration: All reportin
 g and publishing must be completed within six months of the award\n\nMore:
  https://iwmf.submittable.com/submit/c65a15f2-9ff5-4c2b-b2a3-2d897c703381/
 the-kari-howard-fund-for-narrative-journalism-2026?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://iwmf.submittable.com/submit/c65a15f2-9ff5-4c2b-b2a3-2d897c70338
 1/the-kari-howard-fund-for-narrative-journalism-2026?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide\; projects reported and published in English anywhere in
  the world. Open to women and nonbinary journalists.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:oif-artist-mobility-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T204152Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: OIF: Support for Artist Mobility and Circulation of Cultu
 ral Goods 2026 (Francophonie)
DESCRIPTION:The OIF Support for Artist Mobility and Circulation of Cultural
  Goods programme provides grants of up to EUR 5\,000 for mobility (travel\
 , accommodation\, visa fees\, logistics) and up to EUR 2\,500 for circulat
 ion of cultural goods (excess baggage\, freight\, customs) within the Fran
 cophone zone\; both can be combined. ELIGIBILITY: professional artists and
  established artistic collectives with a demonstrated connection to the Fr
 ancophone space (by nationality\, working language\, or artistic collabora
 tions)\, residing in an OIF member state\, with minimum 2 years of experie
 nce in the cultural or artistic sector\; must directly manage the project 
 (not act as intermediaries)\; one application per individual per call. ELI
 GIBLE DISCIPLINES: live performance (music\, theatre\, dance\, comedy\, st
 orytelling)\, cinema/audiovisual\, literature/publishing\, and visual arts
 . PRIORITY given to projects promoting gender equality\, women artists\, c
 reators under 35\, and South-South mobility. Projects must take place betw
 een 25 March and 30 November 2026. Multiple selection sessions throughout 
 the year\; call open until 31 August 2026. Information webinars: 11 March\
 , 13 May\, and 8 July 2026 at 3 PM Paris time. Contact: culture.dlc@franco
 phonie.org. Apply at https://appelsaprojets.francophonie.org/fr/applicatio
 ns/soutien-a-la-circulation-des-biens-et-mobilite-des-artistes.\n\nOrganis
 ation: Organisation internationale de la Francophonie (OIF)\nLocation: Fra
 ncophone zone (OIF member states globally)\nAward: Up to EUR 5\,000 for ar
 tist mobility (travel\, accommodation\, visas\, logistics). Up to EUR 2\,5
 00 for circulation of cultural goods (excess baggage\, freight\, customs).
  Both can be combined. No application fee.\nDuration: Residencies or tours
  of 5 days to 3 months. Projects must take place between 25 March and 30 N
 ovember 2026.\n\nMore: https://appelsaprojets.francophonie.org/fr/applicat
 ions/soutien-a-la-circulation-des-biens-et-mobilite-des-artistes?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://appelsaprojets.francophonie.org/fr/applications/soutien-a-la-ci
 rculation-des-biens-et-mobilite-des-artistes?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Francophone zone (OIF member states globally)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sloan-foundation-book-grants-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T204152Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Alfred P. Sloan Foundation: Book Grants (Public Understan
 ding of Science and Technology)
DESCRIPTION:The one Sloan program that funds individuals directly: grants f
 or authors researching and writing nonfiction books that bring science and
  technology to a general audience. Over 200 authors supported since 1996. 
 Favoured topics include elucidating confusing or controversial scientific 
 subjects\, profiles of figures in science and technology\, science-technol
 ogy-art connections\, women and science\, and the culture and philosophy o
 f science. Start with a letter of inquiry to program director Doron Weber\
 ; full proposals are by invitation only. Reviewed three times a year: LOIs
  by 1 September (decision by 15 February)\, 1 January (by 31 May) and 1 Ma
 y (by 31 October). No application fee.\n\nOrganisation: Alfred P. Sloan Fo
 undation\nLocation: Remote / anywhere (US foundation\; grants paid directl
 y to authors or via a nonprofit host)\nAward: Typically up to $60\,000 per
  book grant\nDuration: Project-based (book research and writing)\n\nMore: 
 https://sloan.org/programs/public-understanding/books?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sloan.org/programs/public-understanding/books?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote / anywhere (US foundation\; grants paid directly to authors
  or via a nonprofit host)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:printed-matter-publisher-work-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T204152Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261004
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261005
SUMMARY:Deadline: Printed Matter Publisher Work Grant 2026
DESCRIPTION:Third annual cycle of Printed Matter's Publisher Work Grant\, s
 upported by Wagner Foundation. Three unrestricted grants of $10\,000 go to
  independent artists' book publishers\, acknowledging past work and provid
 ing capacity-building funds for future book projects and publisher sustain
 ability. ELIGIBILITY: independent imprints with an 'independent spirit\,' 
 generally small in scale\, with a publishing history of one or more years 
 and one or more titles per year\, currently active with future titles in t
 he works\; open to publishers based anywhere (international presses encour
 aged). NOT for individual artists seeking to publish their own work\, nor 
 for commercial presses that exclusively publish client work\; more establi
 shed\, well-funded\, or institutionally supported publishers may be deprio
 ritised. Funds are unrestricted (production\, artists' fees\, equipment\, 
 studio costs\, rent\, etc.). Applications reviewed by Printed Matter and a
  guest jury based on the imprint's body of work\, upcoming projects\, and 
 potential impact. Selected presses also receive a highlighted presence at 
 a 2026 Printed Matter Art Book Fair (travel and accommodation are the gran
 tee's responsibility). Deadline: Sunday 4 October 2026\, 11:59PM EST.\n\nO
 rganisation: Printed Matter\nLocation: New York\, USA-based\; open to publ
 ishers based anywhere in the world\nAward: Three unrestricted grants of $1
 0\,000 USD each\, plus a featured exhibitor presence (complimentary table)
  at a forthcoming Printed Matter NY or LA Art Book Fair. No application fe
 e.\nDuration: Unrestricted\, capacity-building award (no obligation to rea
 lise a particular project)\n\nMore: https://www.printedmatter.org/workgran
 t?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.printedmatter.org/workgrant?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New York\, USA-based\; open to publishers based anywhere in the wo
 rld
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:paul-mellon-centre-author-grants-autumn-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260831T204152Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260914
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260915
SUMMARY:Deadline: Paul Mellon Centre: Author Grants (Autumn 2026 round)
DESCRIPTION:The Paul Mellon Centre funds scholarship and dissemination in B
 ritish art\, visual culture and architectural history\, from the medieval 
 period to the present and across the relevant geographical and cultural co
 ntexts. This is the Autumn 2026 round\, the first under the Centre's restr
 uctured funding scheme announced earlier in 2026\, and all grants in the r
 ound close together on Monday 14 September 2026 at 23:59 BST. ELIGIBILITY:
  the Centre's funding is open to INTERNATIONAL applicants\, not just UK-ba
 sed ones (the exceptions are the MA/MPhil Studentship and Doctoral Scholar
 ship\, which are not part of this round). Every applicant must show how th
 e award advances their research on British art\, visual culture and/or arc
 hitecture. SUPPORT: general application guidance\, an updated FAQ and the 
 Our Funding Focus page set out what the Centre looks for\, and there are w
 riteups of previously successful projects. The grants team will take speci
 fic questions or arrange a call at grants@paul-mellon-centre.ac.uk. No app
 lication fee. THIS GRANT: up to GBP 6\,000 for INDIVIDUALS disseminating r
 esearch on British art through publication\, covering monographs\, edited 
 collections\, catalogues and articles. It funds the practical side of publ
 ishing\, image copyright fees being the example the Centre gives. This is 
 the one grant in the Autumn round aimed at individual authors rather than 
 organisations.\n\nOrganisation: Paul Mellon Centre for Studies in British 
 Art\nLocation: London-based funder. Applications open internationally\; th
 e research must concern British art\, visual culture and/or architectural 
 history.\nAward: Up to GBP 6\,000. No application fee.\nDuration: Autumn 2
 026 round\; applications close Monday 14 September 2026 at 23:59 BST\n\nMo
 re: https://www.paul-mellon-centre.ac.uk/about/news/autumn-round-of-fundin
 g-opportunities-open?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refe
 rral
URL:https://www.paul-mellon-centre.ac.uk/about/news/autumn-round-of-funding
 -opportunities-open?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
LOCATION:London-based funder. Applications open internationally\; the resea
 rch must concern British art\, visual culture and/or architectural history
 .
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
